      "subject": "Guest lecture on agentic programming?",
      "message": "Dear Professor Alvarez,\n\nThank you for reaching out. We would be delighted to have a member of the Jaseci team deliver a 60-minute guest lecture on agentic programming for your graduate seminar.\n\nSeptember 15 works well for us. The 2-4pm Eastern time slot is also fine, and we are happy to participate remotely.\n\nThe lecture will cover the fundamentals of agentic programming using Jaseci, including an overview of the Jac language, the graph-based program structure (nodes, edges, and walkers), and how the framework enables scale-native cloud deployment. We'd also be happy to include a live demo and leave time for student questions.\n\nPlease let us know if you'd like us to tailor the content in any way, and feel free to share the remote meeting link closer to the date.\n\nLooking forward to it.\n\nBest regards,\nThe Jaseci Team\n"
    },
    {
      "to": "priya.nair@devfleet.io",
      "subject": "Quick question | byLLM structured output",
      "message": "Hi Priya,\n\nGreat question \u2014 byLLM does support typed/structured outputs natively, so you won't need to parse the model's text output yourself.\n\nIn byLLM, you can declare a function with a typed return value (including structured objects) and delegate the implementation to an LLM using the `by llm()` syntax. The byLLM runtime then enforces that the model's output conforms to the declared type. So for your log triage pipeline, you could define a structured type with fields for severity, component, and message, and byLLM will handle the parsing and validation automatically.\n\nFor example, you'd define a return type that matches your desired structure (severity | component | message), and the framework ensures the LLM output is mapped into those fields correctly \u2014 no post-processing on your end.\n\nHappy to share a quick code snippet or jump on a call if you'd like to walk through how it would fit into your pipeline.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\nJaseci\n"
    }
  ],
